  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warm and dry today with sunny skies; breezy easterly winds \n   developing this evening.\n\n-  Showers and thunderstorms Thursday into Friday, generally south\n   of Highway 12 and I-90. Storms will be capable of brief heavy\n   rain, small hail, and sudden wind gusts up to 45 mph.\n\n-  Another warming and drying trend Sunday through early next\n   week.\n\nA brief area of high pressure over the region today will bring pleasant,\nsunny, and near-normal temperatures, with highs reaching the 70s in western\nMontana and the 80s in Idaho. Northeast winds of 10 to 15 mph with gusts\naround 20 mph will develop this afternoon. After sunset, winds will\nincrease, bringing easterly wind gusts over 20 to 25 mph, particularly in\nnarrow valleys along and west of the Continental Divide and along the\neastern shores of Flathead Lake.\n\nA low-pressure system will approach southwest Idaho on Thursday, drawing a\nsurge of monsoon moisture into the area. Combined with strong wind shear\n(changing wind speeds and direction with height), this system \nwill trigger thunderstorms capable of producing heavy rain, \nlightning, gusty winds, and small hail. On Friday, afternoon \nheating could increase instability once again, elevating the \nthreat for strong storms capable of producing larger hail (around\n1 inch in size). The highest chances for these thunderstorms will\nfocus across central Idaho and west-central to southwest Montana,\nnorthwest Montana will experience much lower chances for \nthunderstorms, though a few showers are possible. While northwest \nMontana will largely miss out on the activity, sudden wind gusts \nfrom the storms much further south are expected to move into the \nMission and Flathead valleys Thursday evening and Friday. If you \nhave plans to be on Flathead, Swan, or Seeley lakes, expect to \nexperience sudden gusty winds out of the south after 8 PM.\n\nAs the low-pressure system exits the region on Saturday, the focus for\nshowers and thunderstorms will shift east of the US-93 corridor into\ncentral Montana, leaving the lower valleys of Idaho mostly dry with warming\ntemperatures. High pressure will then build across the region from Sunday\ninto early next week. Expect patchy morning fog in river valleys followed\nby afternoon highs potentially reaching the 80s in Missoula and the lower\nvalleys of Idaho. There will also be increasing chances for morning frost\nby mid-week through this weekend. By mid-week, there remains around a 50\npercent chance that a passing cold front could bring breezy conditions and\ncool down the early-week warmth.",
